Q: How to calculate CRC for ISO CAN-FD frames A: <p>The reason why this is so difficult is that nobody in the real world has to manually calculate the CAN bus CRC. This is done in hardware in the processors that support CAN bus communication, so no embedded programmer has to ever care about this.</p>
<p>Additionally the ISO documents where the CRC calculation is explained cannot be downloaded, except you pay for them.</p>
<p>I need this because I'am writing an application that converts an  oscilloscope into a logic analyzer. It loads a capture over USB from the oscilloscope to the computer and has a lot of decoders for UART, SPI, I2C and also CAN bus, that analyze the signal bit by bit.</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/jtKEP7GF.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/jtKEP7GF.png" alt="Oszi Waveform Analyzer - CAN FD decoder" /></a></p>
<p>This allows to find errors on a CAN bus using an oscilloscope. It checks for all possible errors (sample point timing, stuff bits, parity bits, CRC) so I need to manually calculate the CRC for 15 bit, 17 bit and 21 bit CAN bus packets. It took me a long time to get the CRC calculation working correctly.</p>
<p>If you need sample data and working code, download my <strong>open-source project</strong> from <a href="https://netcult.ch/elmue/Oszi-Waveform-Analyzer/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://netcult.ch/elmue/Oszi-Waveform-Analyzer/</a>. It is full of demo files with real CAN bus data captures from CAN classic and CAN FD. All demos result in a correct checksum.</p>
<p>The Python code here in the question is surely not written by an experienced programmer, who would never write bits into an array of 17 integers and roll the array members around. Obviously 17 bits can be written into one integer and calculation can be done by bit shifting and XOR operations, which the processor does in one single instruction.</p>
<p>I wrote a small speed-optimized class in C#, that does the CRC calculation for CAN classic and CAN FD:</p>
<pre class="lang-csharp prettyprint-override"><code>/// &lt;summary&gt;
/// See &quot;Bosch CAN FD.pdf&quot; page 14 and &quot;ISO 11898-1 (2015) CRC.png&quot; 
/// in subfolder &quot;Documentation&quot; of Oszi Waveform Analyzer.
/// &lt;/summary&gt;
class CanCrc
{
    int ms32_CRC, ms32_Poly, ms32_HiBit, ms32_Mask;

    // Constructor
    public CanCrc(CanPacket i_Packet)
    {
        int s32_CrcLen = 15;
        if (i_Packet.IsCanFD)
            s32_CrcLen = i_Packet.ms32_DLC &gt; 16 ? 21 : 17;

        ms32_HiBit =  1 &lt;&lt; (s32_CrcLen - 1); // 15 bit --&gt; 0x4000
        ms32_Mask  = (1 &lt;&lt; s32_CrcLen) - 1;  // 15 bit --&gt; 0x7FFF

        switch (s32_CrcLen)
        {
            case 15: ms32_Poly = 0x004599; ms32_CRC = 0;          break; // CAN classic
            case 17: ms32_Poly = 0x01685B; ms32_CRC = ms32_HiBit; break; // CAN FD
            case 21: ms32_Poly = 0x102899; ms32_CRC = ms32_HiBit; break; // CAN FD
        }
    }

    // Shift-in the next bit
    public void Calculate(bool b_Bit)
    {
        bool b_Highest = (ms32_CRC &amp; ms32_HiBit) &gt; 0;
        ms32_CRC &lt;&lt;= 1;
        if (b_Highest ^ b_Bit)
            ms32_CRC  ^= ms32_Poly; 
    }

    // Get the finished CRC
    public int Finish()
    {
        return ms32_CRC &amp; ms32_Mask;
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to make infinite draggable background using CSS and JavaScript A: <p>You will want to look into css3 flexbox. I have a long code example for you.</p>
<p>This is using the following as the core that makes it work. You can do more CSS stuff to make it more of what you want.</p>
<pre><code>workspace {                
  width: 480px; // whatever you want
  height: 320px; // whatever you want
  overflow: auto;          
}

.flex-content {            
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  min-width: 100%;
  width: max-content;
}

.flex-row {
  display: flex;
}

.item {
  flex: 1 0 140px;
}
</code></pre>
<p>I have full HTML to demonstrate this on, but I ran it through an LLM and it is not formatted, which is not allowed on this site. So I have just put in some handwritten boilerplate that shows how to use it. Flexbox has a grow and a shrink and a basis. You can take a look more deeply here: <a href="https://www.w3schools.com/css/css3_flexbox.asp" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.w3schools.com/css/css3_flexbox.asp</a>. Hope this helps.</p>
<p>Put the the classes on like so, or roughly.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;div id=&quot;workspace&quot; class=&quot;flex-content flex-row item&quot;&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;flex-content&quot;&gt;
        &lt;div class=&quot;flex-row&quot;&gt;
            &lt;div class=&quot;item&quot;&gt;1&lt;/div&gt;
            &lt;!-- etc --&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
        &lt;!-- more rows ... --&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Calling a function accepting T&amp; with an argument of rvalue of type T A: <p>In <code>check_comm(get_line());</code> <code>get_line()</code> returns a temporary <code>std::vector&lt;std::string&gt;</code>.
The rules say that you can only bind <code>const</code> references to temporaries.
<code>check_commands()</code> on the other hand wants to bind a non-<code>const</code> reference to the argument - so compilation fails.</p>
<p>Either make <code>check_commands()</code> bind to a <code>const</code> reference:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>void check_commands(std::vector&lt;std::string&gt; const&amp; line){
//                                           ^^^^^^
</code></pre>
<p>If <code>check_commands()</code> really needs to make changes to <code>line</code>, save the return value from <code>get_line()</code> in a variable to pass in an lvalue instead:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>auto lin = get_line();
check_comm(lin); // now fine
</code></pre>
<p><em>or</em> take the argument by value:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>void check_commands(std::vector&lt;std::string&gt; line){
//                                          ^ no &amp; here
</code></pre>
<p>Note that the latter will make a copy of the whole <code>vector</code> in case you pass in an lvalue, so it's probably not the solution you'd want, but it <em>is</em> an option.</p>

Q: how to refer to class methods in a class variable being a call table list A: <pre><code>
class Instr:
    instrList = [
        [&quot;DATA&quot;, &quot;data_l&quot;],
        [&quot;EQU&quot;, &quot;nul_l&quot;],
        [&quot;LDK&quot;, &quot;two_l&quot;],
        [&quot;LDKL&quot;, &quot;four_l&quot;],
    ]

    def size(self, line):
        instr_name = line[0]
        for instr in self.instrList:
            if instr[0] == instr_name:
                method_name = instr[1]
                method = getattr(self, method_name)
                return method(line)
        raise ValueError(f&quot;Unknown instruction: {instr_name}&quot;)

    def nul_l(self, line=None):
        return 0

    def two_l(self, line=None):
        return 2

    def four_l(self, line=None):
        return 4

    def data_l(self, line):
        count = 0
        items = line[3].split(',')
        for item in items:
            item = item.strip()
            if item.startswith('&quot;') and item.endswith('&quot;'):
                count += len(item) - 2
            else:
                count += 2
        return count

instr_obj = Instr()
line_example = [&quot;DATA&quot;, None, None, '&quot;hi_stackoverflow&quot;,123']
size = instr_obj.size(line_example)
print(&quot;Size:&quot;, size)
</code></pre>

Q: How to use OpenType feature tags with UIFont? A: <p>The other answer does work but seems a bit like an undocumented hack, especially considering the documentation for <code>UIFontDescriptor.AttributeName.featureSettings</code> states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The value is an <strong>array of dictionaries</strong> that represents nondefault font feature settings. Each dictionary contains a <code>type</code> and <code>selector</code> key.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>An a bit more verbose option that also works is passing an array of <code>[String: Any]</code> dictionaries like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>.featureSettings: [
    [
        &quot;CTFeatureOpenTypeTag&quot;: &quot;cv11&quot;,
        &quot;CTFeatureOpenTypeValue&quot;: 1
    ]
]
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>Here is an extension for <code>UIFontDescriptor</code> for adding multiple open type features by their tag:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>extension UIFontDescriptor {
    func addingOpenTypeFeatures&lt;S: Sequence&gt;(_ tags: S) -&gt; UIFontDescriptor where S.Element == String {
        let attributes: [UIFontDescriptor.AttributeName: Any] = [
            .featureSettings: tags.map { tag in
                [&quot;CTFeatureOpenTypeTag&quot;: tag, &quot;CTFeatureOpenTypeValue&quot;: 1]
            }
        ]
        return self.addingAttributes(attributes)
    }
}
</code></pre>
